    "available_at": "2026-10-14T18:17:39.263278+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nForgotten 1966 Dodge Charger Is a Sad Sight, Nice Surprise Inside the Cabin\n13 photos\nPhoto: Facebook Marketplace/Josh Cox\nWhen it comes to the classic Dodge Charger, most people seem to favor the 1968-1971 versions. And I get why: these cars look terrific and can be fast when fitted with big-block V8 engines. But I have a thing for the first-generation Charger . There's something about that fastback design and the \"electric razor\" grille.\nThe original Charger debuted in January 1966, and the company described it as the \"leader of the Dodge rebellion.\" And indeed it was. Not only did it introduce the iconic 426-cubic-inch (7.0-liter) Street Hemi, but it also looked unlike any other B-body Mopar. The fastback design, the upscale interior, and the four-seat layout gave it a unique position on the market.\nBut this recipe didn't catch on; customers were more interested in the more traditional muscle cars. The first-year Charger was relatively successful, with 37,344 units sold, but deliveries dropped to only 15,788 examples in 1967. As a result, Dodge gave the B-body a radical makeover for the 1968 model year.\nNearly 60 years later, the first-gen Charger is nowhere near as desirable as the post-1967 iterations. And surprisingly enough, you can still buy Hemi-equipped examples for under $100,000. As for the non-Hemi cars, most of them are basically doomed to become rust buckets. This 1966 fastback is one of those Chargers struggling for a second chance.\nThe Mopar surfaced in Cartersville, Georgia, and it looks like it's been sitting for a very long time. It was probably parked in this yard decades ago, losing much of its paint due to exposure to the elements. But it's not terrible when it comes to rust, and the interior is almost complete and surprisingly solid.\nThe seller says the vehicle is black, but the body panels show at least three different hues. There's blue paint on the driver-side rear fender and a cream color on the other side. There's some black as well, along with a large amount of surface rust.\nThere's no confirmation on whether the engine is numbers-matching , but the powerplant looks legit. It's a 383-cubic-inch (6.3-liter) V8, which is both good news and bad news. The good news is that it's not a totally undesirable small-block mill. On the other hand, the 383 is not as sought-after as the Hemi or 440-cubic-inch (7.2-liter) RB, either.\nAnd it's not exactly rare. Dodge dropped the four-barrel 383 V8 into 12,328 cars, which is a third of the total 1966-model-year production. However, this Charger hides a nice surprise inside the cabin. It's a manual shifter, which makes this Mopar notably rarer. Specifically, only 2,809 vehicles were equipped with the 383 V8 and four-speed manual combo.\nAnd while this figure is still somewhat big, it's worth considering that many of these cars are in far worse shape. This Charger may actually be worth saving. But is it also worth the $15,000 asking price ? Let me know in the comments section below.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ndodge charger 1966 Dodge Charger Dodge Mopar classic car muscle car barn find yard find\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Volvo EX60 Manufacturing Difficulties Hinder Production Output, US Dealers Hit Worst",
